                "role": "assistant",
                "content": "**Notation**\n\n* Standard state :   \\(p^{\\circ}=1.000\\;\\text{bar}\\)\n* Ideal\u2011gas constant (in the convenient units\u202fL\u202fbar\u202fK\\(^{-1}\\)\u202fmol\\(^{-1}\\))  \n\n\\[\nR = 0.083144626\\;\\frac{\\text{L\u00b7bar}}{\\text{mol\u00b7K}}\n\\]\n\nAll thermodynamic quantities are written as functions of the absolute temperature \\(T\\;( \\text{K})\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Temperature\u2011dependence of the standard state functions  \n\nKirchhoff\u2019s law  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\Delta _r H^{\\circ}(T)=\\Delta _r H^{\\circ}(T_0)+\\int_{T_0}^{T}\\Delta _r C_p^{\\circ}\\,dT'}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand the fundamental relation for entropy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\Delta _r S^{\\circ}(T)=\\Delta _r S^{\\circ}(T_0)+\\int_{T_0}^{T}\\frac{\\Delta _r C_p^{\\circ}}{T'}\\,dT'}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the problem states that \\(\\Delta _r C_p^{\\circ}\\) is **temperature\u2011independent**, the integrals are elementary:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta _r H^{\\circ}(T)&=\\Delta _r H^{\\circ}(T_0)+\\Delta _r C_p^{\\circ}\\,(T-T_0)\\\\[2mm]\n\\Delta _r S^{\\circ}(T)&=\\Delta _r S^{\\circ}(T_0)+\\Delta _r C_p^{\\circ}\\,\n\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac{T}{T_0}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert the numerical data (\\(T_0=298.15\\;\\text{K}\\))\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta _r H^{\\circ}(T)&=15.00\\;\\text{kJ mol}^{-1}\n      -80.00\\;\\frac{\\text{J}}{\\text{mol\u00b7K}}\\;(T-298.15) \\\\[1mm]\n&=38852-80\\,T\\quad\\text{J mol}^{-1}\\\\[2mm]\n\\Delta _r S^{\\circ}(T)&=50.00\\;\\frac{\\text{J}}{\\text{mol\u00b7K}}\n      -80.00\\;\\frac{\\text{J}}{\\text{mol\u00b7K}}\\;\n        \\ln\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{T}{298.15}\\Bigr)\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe standard Gibbs energy follows from the definition  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\Delta _r G^{\\circ}(T)=\\Delta _r H^{\\circ}(T)-T\\,\\Delta _r S^{\\circ}(T)}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFinally the (dimensionless) equilibrium constant based on the standard pressure is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \nK_{p}(T)=\\exp\\!\\Bigl[-\\frac{\\Delta _r G^{\\circ}(T)}{R\\,T}\\Bigr]}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Temperatures for which \\(K_{p}(T)=1\\)\n\n\\(K_{p}=1\\) \u21d4 \\(\\Delta _r G^{\\circ}=0\\) \u21d4 \\(\\Delta _r H^{\\circ}=T\\Delta _r S^{\\circ}\\).\n\nUsing (4),\n\n\\[\n38852-80T = T\\bigl[50-80\\ln(T/298.15)\\bigr].\n\\]\n\nAfter simple algebra\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\ln\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{T}{298.15}\\Bigr)=\\frac{130-\\dfrac{38852}{T}}{80}}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEquation (7) is transcendental; solving it numerically in the interval\n\\(200\\le T\\le1000\\;\\text{K}\\) gives two roots:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_{1}&=300.1\\;\\text{K}\\\\\nT_{2}&=859.5\\;\\text{K}\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\qquad(\\text{to }0.1\\;\\text{K})\n\\]\n\n**Why two solutions?**  \nThe left\u2013hand side of (7) grows only logarithmically with \\(T\\); the right\u2013\nhand side is a straight line minus a term proportional to \\(1/T\\).  \nConsequently the two curves intersect twice, giving two distinct\ntemperatures at which the forward and backward tendencies balance, even\nthough \\(\\Delta _r H^{\\circ}(298.15\\;\\text{K})\\) is positive.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Equilibrium constant at \\(T=500.0\\;\\text{K}\\)\n\nFrom (4)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta _r H^{\\circ}(500) &= 38852-80\\times500 = -1.148\\times10^{3}\\;\\text{J mol}^{-1}\\\\\n\\Delta _r S^{\\circ}(500) &= 50-80\\ln\\!\\Bigl(\\frac{500}{298.15}\\Bigr)\n                         = 50-80(0.514)= 8.88\\;\\frac{\\text{J}}{\\text{mol\u00b7K}}\\\\[1mm]\n\\Delta _r G^{\\circ}(500) &= -1.148\\times10^{3}-500(8.88)\n                         = -5.588\\times10^{3}\\;\\text{J mol}^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nInsert into (6) (using \\(R=8.3144626\\;\\text{J mol}^{-1}\\text{K}^{-1}\\))\n\n\\[\nK_{p}(500)=\\exp\\!\\Bigl[\\frac{5.588\\times10^{3}}{8.3144626\\times500}\\Bigr]\n          =\\exp(1.344)=\\boxed{3.84}\n\\]\n\n(three significant figures)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constant\u2011pressure reactor (total pressure kept at 5\u202fbar)\n\nInitial amounts  \n\n\\[\nn_{\\mathrm A}^{0}=1.000\\;\\text{mol},\\qquad\nn_{\\text{inert}}^{0}=1.000\\;\\text{mol},\\qquad\nn_{\\mathrm B}^{0}=n_{\\mathrm C}^{0}=0 .\n\\]\n\nLet the forward extent be \\(\\xi_{P}\\).  \nBecause the total pressure is imposed, the volume adjusts according to the ideal\u2011gas law, therefore the **mole fractions** are sufficient to obtain the partial pressures:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{\\mathrm A}&=1-\\xi_{P},\\\\\nn_{\\mathrm B}&=n_{\\mathrm C}= \\xi_{P},\\\\\nn_{\\text{inert}}&=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTotal moles  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\!tot}=2+\\xi_{P}.\n\\]\n\nPartial pressures (ideal gas, \\(p^{\\circ}=1\\;\\text{bar}\\))\n\n\\[\np_i = y_i\\,P_{\\!tot}=y_i\\,5\\;\\text{bar},\n\\qquad y_i=\\frac{n_i}{N_{\\!tot}} .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\np_{\\mathrm A}= \\frac{1-\\xi_{P}}{2+\\xi_{P}}\\;5,\\qquad\np_{\\mathrm B}=p_{\\mathrm C}= \\frac{\\xi_{P}}{2+\\xi_{P}}\\;5 .\n\\]\n\nThe reaction quotient (dimensionless) is  \n\n\\[\nQ=\\frac{p_{\\mathrm B}p_{\\mathrm C}}{p_{\\mathrm A}}\n   =\\frac{5\\xi_{P}^{2}}{(2+\\xi_{P})(1-\\xi_{P})}.\n\\]\n\nAt equilibrium \\(Q=K_{p}(500)=3.84\\).  The governing equation for \\(\\xi_{P}\\) is therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n5\\,\\xi_{P}^{2}=3.84\\,(1-\\xi_{P})(2+\\xi_{P}) }\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSolving (8):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\xi_{P}^{2}&=3.84(2-\\xi_{P}-\\xi_{P}^{2})\\\\\n8.837\\,\\xi_{P}^{2}+3.837\\,\\xi_{P}-7.674&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\xi_{P}= \\frac{-3.837+\\sqrt{3.837^{2}+4(8.837)(7.674)}}{2(8.837)}\n       =\\boxed{0.739\\;\\text{mol}}\n\\]\n\n(The other mathematical root is negative and therefore non\u2011physical.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Rigid\u2011volume reactor (initial pressure 5\u202fbar, volume fixed)\n\nInitial total moles = 2 \u21d2 the volume that gives a pressure of 5\u202fbar at 500\u202fK is  \n\n\\[\nV=\\frac{nRT}{P}= \\frac{2\\,R\\,T}{5\\;\\text{bar}}\n   =\\frac{2\\times0.083144626\\times500}{5}=16.628\\;\\text{L}.\n\\]\n\nWhen the reaction proceeds to an extent \\(\\xi_{V}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{\\mathrm A}&=1-\\xi_{V},\\\\\nn_{\\mathrm B}=n_{\\mathrm C}&=\\xi_{V},\\\\\nn_{\\text{inert}}&=1 ,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad\nN_{\\!tot}=2+\\xi_{V}.\n\\]\n\nWith a **fixed volume**, the instantaneous total pressure is  \n\n\\[\nP=\\frac{N_{\\!tot}RT}{V}.\n\\]\n\nPartial pressures are therefore  \n\n\\[\np_{\\mathrm A}= \\frac{1-\\xi_{V}}{V}RT,\\qquad\np_{\\mathrm B}=p_{\\mathrm C}= \\frac{\\xi_{V}}{V}RT .\n\\]\n\nThe reaction quotient becomes  \n\n\\[\nQ=\\frac{p_{\\mathrm B}p_{\\mathrm C}}{p_{\\mathrm A}}\n   =\\frac{\\xi_{V}^{2}RT}{V(1-\\xi_{V})}.\n\\]\n\nSetting \\(Q=K_{p}=3.84\\) gives the equilibrium condition for \\(\\xi_{V}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\xi_{V}^{2}\\,RT = 3.84\\,V\\,(1-\\xi_{V}) }\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInsert the numbers (\\(R=0.083144626\\;\\text{L\u00b7bar\u00b7K}^{-1}\\text{mol}^{-1},\n\\;T=500\\;\\text{K},\\;V=16.628\\;\\text{L}\\)):\n\n\\[\n41.57\\,\\xi_{V}^{2}=63.66\\,(1-\\xi_{V})\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n41.57\\,\\xi_{V}^{2}+63.66\\,\\xi_{V}-63.66=0 .\n\\]\n\nSolution:\n\n\\[\n\\xi_{V}= \\frac{-63.66+\\sqrt{63.66^{2}+4(41.57)(63.66)}}{2(41.57)}\n        =\\boxed{0.684\\;\\text{mol}} .\n\\]\n\n(The second root is negative and discarded.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Comparison and physical interpretation  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\xi_{P}=0.739\\;\\text{mol}\\qquad\n       \\xi_{V}=0.684\\;\\text{mol}}\n\\]\n\nThe forward reaction produces **one mole of gaseous product per mole of reactant consumed** (\\(\\Delta\\nu = +1\\)).  \nAccording to Le\u202fChatelier\u2019s principle, an increase in the total pressure tends to shift the equilibrium toward the side with **fewer gas molecules**; therefore a high pressure suppresses the forward reaction.\n\n* **Constant\u2011pressure reactor:**  \n  The system is allowed to expand its volume as the number of moles grows.  \n  The pressure stays at the prescribed 5\u202fbar, so the partial pressure of the reactant \\(A\\) falls relatively slowly while the product partial pressures rise.  Consequently a larger amount of \\(A\\) can be converted before the reaction quotient reaches the constant \\(K_{p}\\); the equilibrium lies farther to the right, giving the larger extent \\(\\xi_{P}=0.739\\)\u202fmol.\n\n* **Rigid\u2011volume reactor:**  \n  When the volume is fixed, every additional mole of product raises the total pressure.  \n  The rising pressure makes the denominator of the reaction quotient (the partial pressure of \\(A\\)) increase more rapidly, so the system \u201cfeels\u2019\u2019 a higher effective pressure and the forward reaction is penalised.  Equilibrium is reached at a smaller conversion, \\(\\xi_{V}=0.684\\)\u202fmol.\n\nMathematically the difference originates from the distinct forms of the reaction quotient:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{Const.\\;P:}&\\qquad Q(P)=\\frac{5\\,\\xi^{2}}{(2+\\xi)(1-\\xi)}\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{Const.\\;V:}&\\qquad Q(V)=\\frac{\\xi^{2}RT}{V(1-\\xi)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth equations are solved for the same \\(K_{p}\\); because the factor multiplying \\(\\xi^{2}\\) is larger in the constant\u2011pressure case (the \u201c5\u201d versus the much smaller \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{RT}{V}= \\frac{41.57}{16.628}=2.50\\)), a larger \\(\\xi\\) is required to attain the same value of \\(Q\\).  This quantitative picture mirrors the qualitative Le\u202fChatelier argument presented above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Summary**\n\n| Quantity | Expression / value |\n|---|---|\n| \\(\\Delta_r H^{\\circ}(T)\\) | \\(38852-80T\\;\\text{J mol}^{-1}\\) |\n| \\(\\Delta_r S^{\\circ}(T)\\) | \\(50-80\\ln(T/298.15)\\;\\text{J mol}^{-1}\\text{K}^{-1}\\) |\n| \\(\\Delta_r G^{\\circ}(T)\\) | \\(\\Delta_r H^{\\circ}(T)-T\\Delta_r S^{\\circ}(T)\\) |\n| \\(K_p(T)\\) | \\(\\exp[-\\Delta_r G^{\\circ}(T)/(RT)]\\) |\n| \\(K_p=1\\) temperatures | \\(T_1=300.1\\;\\text{K},\\;T_2=859.5\\;\\text{K}\\) |\n| \\(K_p(500\\;\\text{K})\\) | \\(3.84\\) |\n| Extent at constant\u202fP (5\u202fbar) | \\(\\xi_P=0.739\\;\\text{mol}\\) |\n| Extent at constant\u202fV (initial 5\u202fbar) | \\(\\xi_V=0.684\\;\\text{mol}\\) |\n\nThese results satisfy all parts of the problem and illustrate how the same chemical equilibrium behaves differently under imposed pressure versus fixed\u2011volume conditions.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to produce detailed solution steps.\n\nFirst part: Derive expressions using Kirchhoff's law: \u0394H\u00b0(T) = \u0394H\u00b0(T0) + \u222b_{T0}^{T} \u0394Cp dT' . Since \u0394Cp constant, integrate: \u0394H\u00b0(T) = \u0394H\u00b0(T0) + \u0394Cp (T - T0).\n\nSimilarly, \u0394S\u00b0(T) = \u0394S\u00b0(T0) + \u222b_{T0}^{T} \u0394Cp/T' dT' = \u0394S\u00b0(T0) + \u0394Cp ln(T/T0).\n\nThen \u0394G\u00b0(T) = \u0394H\u00b0(T) - T \u0394S\u00b0(T). Also Kp = exp(-\u0394G\u00b0/RT). Provide dimensionless Kp (i.e., activities based on p\u00b0). Use ideal gases: Kp = (p_B/p\u00b0)(p_C/p\u00b0)/(p_A/p\u00b0) = (p_B p_C)/ (p_A p\u00b0). But we can express via equilibrium constant expression.\n\nSecond part: Solve Kp(T)=1 => \u0394G\u00b0(T)=0 => \u0394H\u00b0(T) = T \u0394S\u00b0(T). Plug expressions: \u0394H\u00b0(T0)+\u0394Cp (T-T0) = T[\u0394S\u00b0(T0)+\u0394Cp ln(T/T0)]. This transcendental equation may have up to two solutions because RHS includes log term; left side linear. Graphically there could be two intersections. Solve numerically between 200-1000 K.\n\nLet's compute approximate values.\n\nGiven numbers:\n\n\u0394H0 = 15.00 kJ/mol = 15000 J/mol.\n\u0394S0 = 50.00 J/(mol K).\n\u0394Cp = -80.00 J/(mol K).\n\nT0 = 298.15 K.\n\nCompute \u0394H(T) = 15000 + (-80)*(T-298.15) = 15000 -80(T-298.15).\n\nSimplify: = 15000 -80T + 80*298.15 = 15000 -80T + 23852 = 38852 -80T.\n\nCheck: 80*298.15 = 23852. So \u0394H(T) = 38852 - 80T J/mol.\n\n\u0394S(T) = 50 + (-80)*ln(T/298.15) = 50 -80 ln(T/298.15).\n\nNow set \u0394G=0: \u0394H = T \u0394S.\n\nEquation: 38852 - 80T = T [50 -80 ln(T/298.15)].\n\n=> 38852 - 80T = 50T -80 T ln(T/298.15)\n\nBring terms: 38852 - 80T -50T = -80 T ln(T/298.15)\n\n=> 38852 -130T = -80 T ln(T/298.15)\n\nMultiply both sides by -1: -38852 +130T = 80 T ln(T/298.15)\n\n=> (130T -38852) = 80 T ln(T/298.15)\n\nDivide both sides by T (T>0): 130 - 38852/T = 80 ln(T/298.15)\n\nThus: 80 ln(T/298.15) = 130 - 38852/T\n\n=> ln(T/298.15) = (130 - 38852/T)/80\n\n=> T/298.15 = exp[(130 - 38852/T)/80]\n\n=> Equation to solve numerically.\n\nWe can attempt approximate solutions.\n\nLet f(T) = exp[(130 - 38852/T)/80] - T/298.15 =0.\n\nWe'll search.\n\nAt low T ~200 K:\nCompute 38852/200 =194.26. Then (130 -194.26)/80 = (-64.26)/80 = -0.80325. Exp = e^{-0.803}=0.447. T/298.15 =200/298.15=0.671. f =0.447-0.671=-0.224 negative.\n\nAt high T ~1000 K:\n38852/1000=38.852. (130-38.852)/80 =91.148/80=1.13935. Exp =3.124. T/298.15=1000/298.15=3.354. f=3.124-3.354=-0.230 negative. So both ends negative? Wait maybe there are two roots where f changes sign positive somewhere.\n\nCheck around T=300 K:\n38852/300=129.5067. (130-129.5067)/80=0.4933/80=0.006166. Exp\u22481.00618. T/298.15=300/298.15=1.00621. f\u22481.00618-1.00621=-0.00003 approx zero! So T\u2248300 K is root (makes sense because at T0, \u0394G maybe small? Actually at T0, \u0394G = \u0394H0 - T0 \u0394S0 =15000 - 298.15*50 =15000 -14907.5 =92.5 J >0, not zero. Hmm but our derived equation gave near zero due to rounding. Let's compute precisely.\n\nCompute \u0394G at T0: 15000 - 298.15*50 =15000 -14907.5 =92.5 J. Not zero. So maybe my earlier expression mis-evaluated. Let's compute f at T0 exactly:\n\nT=298.15: 38852/T =38852/298.15 =129.999? Let's calculate: 298.15*130 = 38759.5, close. Actually 298.15*130 = 38759.5, not 38852. So 38852/298.15 = 129.99... Let's compute: 38852 / 298.15 = 129.999... Indeed 298.15*130 = 38759.5, difference 92.5. So 38852 = 38759.5 +92.5. So dividing gives 130 + 92.5/298.15 \u2248130+0.310 =130.31. Wait that's wrong. Let's compute accurately: 38852 / 298.15 = ?\n\nUse calculator mental: 298.15 * 130 = 38759.5. Subtract from 38852 gives 92.5. So 38852 = 38759.5 +92.5. Therefore 38852/298.15 = 130 + 92.5/298.15 =130 +0.310 =130.310. Yes.\n\nThus (130 - 38852/T) = 130 -130.31 = -0.310. Divide by 80 => -0.003875. Exp = e^{-0.003875}=0.99614. T/298.15 =1.0. f =0.99614 -1 = -0.00386 negative. So still negative.\n\nSo function negative near T0. Need positive region somewhere else.\n\nCheck T=400 K:\n38852/400=97.13. (130-97.13)=32.87. /80=0.4109. Exp=1.508. T/298.15=400/298.15=1.342. f=1.508-1.342=0.166 positive. So root between 300 and 400.\n\nCheck T=350 K:\n38852/350=111.006. (130-111.006)=18.994. /80=0.23743. Exp=1.2676. T/298.15=350/298.15=1.174. f=1.2676-1.174=0.0936 positive.\n\nT=320 K:\n38852/320=121.4125. (130-121.4125)=8.5875. /80=0.10734. Exp=1.1133. T/298.15=320/298.15=1.0736. f=1.1133-1.0736=0.0397 positive.\n\nT=310 K:\n38852/310=125.33. (130-125.33)=4.67. /80=0.05838. Exp=1.0601. T/298.15=310/298.15=1.040. f=1.0601-1.040=0.0201 positive.\n\nT=305 K:\n38852/305=127.41. (130-127.41)=2.59. /80=0.032375. Exp=1.03296. T/298.15=305/298.15=1.023. f=1.03296-1.023=0.00996 positive.\n\nT=302 K:\n38852/302=128.66. (130-128.66)=1.34. /80=0.01675. Exp=1.01687. T/298.15=302/298.15=1.013. f=1.01687-1.013=0.00387 positive.\n\nT=301 K:\n38852/301=129.11. (130-129.11)=0.89. /80=0.011125. Exp=1.01120. T/298.15=301/298.15=1.0096. f=1.01120-1.0096=0.0016 positive.\n\nT=300.5 K:\n38852/300.5=129.30. (130-129.30)=0.70. /80=0.00875. Exp=1.00878. T/298.15=300.5/298.15=1.0079. f=1.00878-1.0079=0.00088 positive.\n\nT=300.1 K:\n38852/300.1=129.45. diff=0.55. /80=0.006875. Exp=1.00689. Ratio=300.1/298.15=1.00668. f=1.00689-1.00668=0.00021 positive.\n\nT=300.05 K:\ndiff about 0.45? Let's compute quickly: 38852/300.05=129.51. diff=0.49. /80=0.006125. Exp=1.00615. ratio=300.05/298.15=1.00634. f=1.00615-1.00634=-0.00019 negative.\n\nThus root near 300.1 K. So first root ~300.1 K.\n\nNow check for second root above 400 K.\n\nAt T=600 K:\n38852/600=64.753. (130-64.753)=65.247. /80=0.81559. Exp=2.260. Ratio=600/298.15=2.012. f=2.260-2.012=0.248 positive.\n\nAt T=800 K:\n38852/800=48.565. (130-48.565)=81.435. /80=1.01794. Exp=2.766. Ratio=800/298.15=2.684. f=2.766-2.684=0.082 positive.\n\nAt T=900 K:\n38852/900=43.169. diff=86.831. /80=1.08539. Exp=2.960. Ratio=900/298.15=3.019. f=2.960-3.019=-0.059 negative.\n\nThus root between 800 and 900 K.\n\nFind more precise: T=850 K:\n38852/850=45.708. diff=84.292. /80=1.05365. Exp=2.868. Ratio=850/298.15=2.852. f=2.868-2.852=0.016.\n\nT=870 K:\n38852/870=44.6586. diff=85.3414. /80=1.06677. Exp=2.905. Ratio=870/298.15=2.919. f=2.905-2.919=-0.014.\n\nRoot near 860 K.\n\nT=860 K:\n38852/860=45.235. diff=84.765. /80=1.05956. Exp=2.884. Ratio=860/298.15=2.885. f=2.884-2.885\u2248 -0.001.\n\nClose. Slightly negative. Try 859 K:\n38852/859=45.267. diff=84.733. /80=1.05916. Exp=2.883. Ratio=859/298.15=2.884. f\u2248 -0.001.\n\nTry 858 K:\n38852/858=45.301. diff=84.699. /80=1.05874. Exp=2.882. Ratio=858/298.15=2.883. f\u2248 -0.001.\n\nSeems root around 857 K maybe positive.\n\nT=857 K:\n38852/857=45.337. diff=84.663. /80=1.05833. Exp=2.881. Ratio=857/298.15=2.882. f\u2248 -0.001.\n\nHmm all negative after 860. At 855 K we got positive 0.016. At 860 negative -0.001. So root near 858 K maybe.\n\nInterpolate linearly between 855 (f=+0.016) and 860 (f=-0.001). Root approx at 855 + (0.016)/(0.016+0.001)*(5) \u2248855 + (0.016/0.017)*5 \u2248855 + 4.706 \u2248859.7 K.\n\nCheck 860 gave -0.001, okay.\n\nLet's compute more precisely using maybe 860.5 K.\n\nT=860.5:\n38852/860.5=45.247. diff=84.753. /80=1.05942. Exp=2.885. Ratio=860.5/298.15=2.886. f\u2248 -0.001.\n\nThus root ~859.5 K.\n\nLet's compute at 859.5 K:\n38852/859.5=45.254. diff=84.746. /80=1.05933. Exp=2.885. Ratio=859.5/298.15=2.885. f\u22480? Good.\n\nThus second root ~859.5 K.\n\nTherefore temperatures where Kp=1 are approximately 300.1 K and 859.5 K.\n\nExplain multiple roots due to monotonic linear vs logarithmic functions crossing twice.\n\nThird part: compute Kp at 500 K.\n\nFirst compute \u0394H(500) = 38852 -80*500 = 38852 -40000 = -1148 J/mol = -1.148 kJ.\n\n\u0394S(500) = 50 -80 ln(500/298.15). Compute ln(500/298.15)=ln(1.677) \u22480.514. Multiply -80 => -80*0.514 = -41.12. So \u0394S = 50 -41.12 = 8.88 J/K.\n\n\u0394G = \u0394H - T\u0394S = -1148 - 500*8.88 = -1148 - 4440 = -5588 J.\n\nKp = exp(-\u0394G/(RT)) = exp(5588/(8.3144626*500)). Compute denominator: 8.3144626*500 = 4157.2313. Ratio = 5588/4157.23 = 1.344. exp(1.344)=3.837. To three sig figs: 3.84.\n\nAlternatively compute directly.\n\nThus Kp \u22483.84.\n\nFourth part: System at constant total pressure 5 bar, initial moles: n_A0=1, n_inert=1, n_B0=n_C0=0. Reaction proceeds with extent \u03be_P (since pressure controlled). For ideal gases, total moles N_total = n_A + n_inert + n_B + n_C = (1-\u03be)+(1)+(\u03be)+(\u03be) = 2 + \u03be. Because A consumed \u03be, produce B and C each \u03be.\n\nTotal pressure P_tot =5 bar constant. Volume will adjust to keep P constant: V = (N_total RT)/P_tot.\n\nPartial pressures: p_i = y_i * P_tot, where y_i = n_i/N_total.\n\nThus p_A = ((1-\u03be)/ (2+\u03be)) *5 ; p_B = (\u03be/(2+\u03be))*5 ; p_C same.\n\nReaction quotient Q = (p_B p_C)/(p_A p\u00b0)?? Actually Kp uses dimensionless activities relative to p\u00b0, but we can write Q = (p_B/p\u00b0)*(p_C/p\u00b0)/(p_A/p\u00b0) = (p_B p_C)/(p_A p\u00b0). Since p\u00b0=1 bar, Q = (p_B p_C)/p_A.\n\nPlug partials: Q = [ (\u03be/(2+\u03be))*5 * (\u03be/(2+\u03be))*5 ] / [ ((1-\u03be)/(2+\u03be))*5 ] = [ (\u03be^2 *25)/(2+\u03be)^2 ] / [ (1-\u03be)*5/(2+\u03be) ] = (\u03be^2 *25)/(2+\u03be)^2 * (2+\u03be)/(5(1-\u03be)) = (\u03be^2 *25)/(5(1-\u03be)(2+\u03be)).\n\nSimplify: Q = (5 \u03be^2)/((1-\u03be)(2+\u03be)).\n\nSet Q = Kp(500) = 3.837 (approx). Solve equation:\n\n5 \u03be^2 = 3.837 (1-\u03be)(2+\u03be).\n\nExpand RHS: (1-\u03be)(2+\u03be) = 2 + \u03be -2\u03be - \u03be^2 = 2 - \u03be - \u03be^2? Wait compute: (1-\u03be)*(2+\u03be) = 1*2 +1*\u03be - \u03be*2 - \u03be*\u03be = 2 + \u03be -2\u03be - \u03be^2 = 2 - \u03be - \u03be^2.\n\nThus RHS = 3.837 (2 - \u03be - \u03be^2) = 7.674 -3.837 \u03be -3.837 \u03be^2.\n\nEquation: 5 \u03be^2 = 7.674 -3.837 \u03be -3.837 \u03be^2.\n\nBring all terms: 5 \u03be^2 +3.837 \u03be^2 = 7.674 -3.837 \u03be.\n\n=> 8.837 \u03be^2 +3.837 \u03be -7.674 =0.\n\nSolve quadratic: a=8.837, b=3.837, c=-7.674.\n\nDiscriminant D = b^2 -4ac = (3.837)^2 -4*8.837*(-7.674) = 14.72 + 4*8.837*7.674.\n\nCompute 8.837*7.674 \u2248 67.78. Times 4 = 271.12. Add 14.72 => 285.84.\n\nsqrt(D) \u2248 16.90.\n\nSolutions: \u03be = [-b \u00b1 sqrt(D)]/(2a) = [-3.837 \u00b1 16.90]/(17.674).\n\nPositive root: (-3.837 +16.90)=13.063 /17.674 =0.739.\n\nNegative root: (-3.837 -16.90) = -20.737 /17.674 = -1.173 (nonphysical because \u03be cannot be negative given initial B,C zero and forward direction favored? Could be negative if reverse but not allowed because can't exceed initial A? Actually \u03be negative would mean consumption of B and C producing A, but they start zero, so not possible.)\n\nThus \u03be_P \u22480.739 mol.\n\nCheck that \u03be \u22641 (cannot consume more A than present), satisfied.\n\nFifth part: Rigid vessel case: volume fixed V determined by initial condition: initial total moles =2 (A+inert). At T=500 K, initial pressure 5 bar. So V = nRT/P = 2 *R*500 /5 = (1000 R)/5 =200 R. Numerically: R=8.3144626 J/mol\u00b7K, so V =200*8.3144626 =1662.8925 L\u00b7bar? Actually units: J = Pa\u00b7m\u00b3, but bar*L conversion factor 1 bar\u00b7L = 100 J. Anyway we just need expression.\n\nDuring reaction, total moles become N_total = 2+\u03be (same as before). Pressure will increase: P = (N_total RT)/V.\n\nPartial pressures: p_i = y_i * P.\n\nBut easier: p_A = (n_A/N_total) * P = ((1-\u03be)/(2+\u03be))*P.\n\nSince P = (N_total RT)/V = (2+\u03be) RT / V.\n\nThus p_A = ((1-\u03be)/(2+\u03be))* (2+\u03be) RT / V = (1-\u03be) RT / V.\n\nSimilarly p_B = \u03be RT / V ; p_C = \u03be RT / V.\n\nHence reaction quotient Q = (p_B p_C)/p_A = (\u03be^2 RT^2 / V^2) / ((1-\u03be) RT / V) = (\u03be^2 RT / V) / (1-\u03be) = (\u03be^2 RT)/(V (1-\u03be)).\n\nSet equal to Kp = 3.837.\n\nThus equation: \u03be^2 RT = 3.837 V (1-\u03be).\n\nRecall V = 2 RT / P_initial? Wait initial pressure 5 bar corresponds to initial N_initial=2, so V = n_i RT / P_i = 2 RT /5 bar. Convert bar to Pa? But we can keep consistent units: Use same R and pressure unit bar, but note that Kp dimensionless uses p\u00b0=1 bar, so we should use pressures in bar. Our expression for p_i using ideal gas law with R expressed per bar? Usually R = 0.08314 L\u00b7bar\u00b7K\u207b\u00b9\u00b7mol\u207b\u00b9. However we used J and bar incorrectly. Better to use R' = 0.08314 L\u00b7bar\u00b7K\u207b\u00b9\u00b7mol\u207b\u00b9 to keep pressures in bar. Let's adopt that to avoid confusion.\n\nThus V = 2 *0.08314*500 /5 = 0.08314*1000/5 = 0.08314*200 = 16.628 L.\n\nNow use this V.\n\nEquation: \u03be^2 *R*T = Kp * V *(1-\u03be). Using R=0.08314 L\u00b7bar\u00b7K\u207b\u00b9\u00b7mol\u207b\u00b9, T=500 K.\n\nCompute RT = 0.08314*500 = 41.57 bar\u00b7L\u00b7mol\u207b\u00b9.\n\nThus left side: \u03be^2 *41.57.\n\nRight side: 3.837 * V *(1-\u03be) = 3.837 *16.628*(1-\u03be) = 63.66*(1-\u03be).\n\nEquation: 41.57 \u03be^2 = 63.66 (1-\u03be).\n\nRearrange: 41.57 \u03be^2 +63.66 \u03be -63.66 =0.\n\nSolve quadratic: a=41.57, b=63.66, c=-63.66.\n\nDiscriminant: b^2 -4ac = (63.66)^2 -4*41.57*(-63.66) = 4054.5 + 10471.5 =14526.0.\n\nsqrt =120.55.\n\nSolutions: \u03be = [-63.66 \u00b1120.55]/(2*41.57)=[-63.66 \u00b1120.55]/83.14.\n\nPositive root: (-63.66+120.55)=56.89 /83.14 =0.684.\n\nNegative root: (-63.66-120.55)=-184.21/83.14 = -2.215 (reject).\n\nThus \u03be_V \u22480.684 mol.\n\nSixth part: Compare \u03be_P (0.739) > \u03be_V (0.684). Explanation: At constant total pressure, when reaction consumes A and produces B & C, total moles increase (\u0394\u03bd = +1). According to Le Chatelier, increasing pressure shifts equilibrium toward side with fewer moles; here reaction increases moles, so higher pressure suppresses forward reaction. In the constant-pressure case, the system can expand volume as moles increase, keeping pressure constant, allowing more forward progress (higher \u03be). In the rigid-volume case, pressure rises when moles increase, which opposes further reaction, limiting extent. Hence larger \u03be under constant pressure.\n\nProvide quantitative comparison and link to reaction quotient: Q depends differently on \u03be due to different dependence of p_A etc. Under constant pressure, p_A decreases slower, leading to larger Q for given \u03be, requiring larger \u03be to reach Kp.\n\nNow craft final answer with clear derivations.\n\n"
